{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"1b3d6072575ae74ae51bc6cad6b69270af3b89cae367859d6191721c3a3e9662","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_e337d1c5642788931b1ed2ed036d70463803a82084fa46b401cba7051637b6b0","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a significant contribution to the intersection of computer vision and neuroscience by providing a quantitative, controlled comparison between human psychophysical data and DNN computations. Its primary significance lies in validating DNNs as a tool for modeling human perception and engineering perceptual loss metrics, with results that are robust across architectures and consistent with theoretical convergence hypotheses.","paper_id":"BJbD_Pqlg","paper_identity_sha256":"0790c709945235ee140eb70337d238d3bba6d80d1da53500bcb9bc391f42f27c","prompt_identity_sha256":"9c022d67c9b7c366c29fd278b8db077153f4ecabce0c03e3ac149c15079f03b9","raw_response_sha256":"79704642df370b8f5071273cd45cbd2b7d7a659411d70dceab106b49f080322c","request_identity_sha256":"418a1fe05cd89030ce98f9747277f3f80778c18eb95d4a16be1f6cdccb95c9d0","reviewer_identity_sha256":"32b29c26fbf1508e066dfb25b1a9a3fe0654c5869a7da3020e1e8e61940befe3","role":"SIGN","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"fb047d5c0786a9198df53d8fa251b7acc32c48d4f168f2a5c000c4bbf0941fb0","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-001","SRC-005","SRC-027"],"review_text":"The work addresses a broadly important problem by investigating the computational convergence between artificial deep neural networks and biological visual systems. By framing the study around the hypothesis that different learning regimes may converge to similar optimal computations, the paper contributes to a fundamental question in both machine learning and neuroscience. The findings support the theoretical view that human perception properties may be a consequence of architecture-independent visual learning, a perspective that has the potential to influence multiple research areas, including brain modeling and the design of general-purpose visual algorithms.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-BRD"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-028","SRC-029"],"review_text":"The paper demonstrates clear domain impact in two specific areas: brain modeling and engineering. For brain modeling, the results provide a controlled condition to support the use of DNNs as a framework for understanding biological vision, offering a more accurate alternative to less general biologically plausible simulations. In the engineering domain, the paper quantifies the applicability of DNN computation for estimating perceptual loss, suggesting practical utility in tasks such as image compression where minimizing visual artifacts is critical. The demonstration of a 'zero-cost first-order approximation' for psychophysical outcomes further highlights its value for researchers in these fields.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-DOM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-010","SRC-013"],"review_text":"The paper reports substantial improvements over simple baselines and achieves performance on par with complex, parameter-heavy perceptual models. Specifically, the DNN-based L1 metric explains approximately 60% of the perceptual variability, significantly outperforming simple image statistical properties like RMS contrast (which explains only 27%). The Spearman rank-order correlation (SROCC) of the DNN metric (approx. 0.79) is also higher than that of the detailed perceptual model (0.70). These results are consistent across multiple DNN architectures (VGG-19, GoogLeNet, CaffeNet, ResNet-152), indicating that the improvement is robust and not an artifact of a specific model design.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-SOT"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-029"],"review_text":"The potential real-world impact is primarily situated in the engineering of visual systems, particularly in image processing and compression. By providing a metric that is 'far superior to simple image statistical properties' for estimating the saliency of image changes, the work offers a pathway to developing algorithms that better preserve human-perceived quality during degradation. This could lead to more efficient and visually pleasing image compression standards. However, the paper does not extensively discuss broader societal ramifications, such as fairness or environmental impacts, focusing instead on the technical and scientific utility of the perceptual metric.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-IMP"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"24d150f59ccfe95e1a94270d7d8895d0f1a780241b629f7721f3fe7febce09bc","paper_id":"BJbD_Pqlg","paper_ordinal":33,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"79704642df370b8f5071273cd45cbd2b7d7a659411d70dceab106b49f080322c","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"BJbD_Pqlg","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"6c8d1243ab5b508f1e1f1d8389ae78e9456199a4f6b84195ae3ee507947d4716","request_identity_sha256":"418a1fe05cd89030ce98f9747277f3f80778c18eb95d4a16be1f6cdccb95c9d0","role":"SIGN","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v6","usage":{"completion_tokens":808,"prompt_tokens":18961,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":19769},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"418a1fe05cd89030ce98f9747277f3f80778c18eb95d4a16be1f6cdccb95c9d0","role":"SIGN","role_ordinal":2,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}